<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benjamin of Tudela</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1173">1173</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Benjamin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a> was a Jewish traveller from Tudela in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> who journeyed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> through southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, probably between about 1165 and 1173. His Book of Travels, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, describes the Jewish communities he found and gives valuable information on the cities, trade and rulers of the twelfth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benjamin-of-tudela.jpg" alt="Benjamin of Tudela" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jialxv · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benjam%C3%ADn_de_Tudela_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Benjamin's personal life. He was a native of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre</a> and lived in the second half of the 12th century, and his father's name was Jonah. Non-Jewish sources often call him Rabbi, but there is no reliable evidence that he held that title. He is credited with a broad education and a knowledge of many languages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Benjamin set out from the north-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> around 1165, in what may have begun as a pilgrimage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>. He may also have had a commercial motive, since he shows interest in the coral trade, or he may have meant to list Jewish communities on the way to the Land of Israel as a guide to where travellers could find hospitality. Scholars do not agree on his exact route. He began at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, went down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarragona">Tarragona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girona">Girona</a>, and then north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> before sailing from Marseille.</p><p>After visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, Lucca, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, then crossed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, the Land of Israel and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, which he called Shinar, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. From there he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, then crossed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> in 1173. He visited some 300 cities, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, Sura and Pumbedita, and gathered information on places he only heard of, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. His visit to the ruins outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> is one of the earliest accurate descriptions of the site of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Benjamin described his journey in a book written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, The Travels of Benjamin, also called the Book of Travels. It describes the countries he visited with an emphasis on Jewish communities, giving their populations and the names of notable leaders, and it describes local customs, Jewish and non-Jewish, with an emphasis on urban life. He wrote of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> with great enthusiasm and often notes the respect and mixing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. He also describes the Al-Hashishin, and a significant Jewish community somewhere around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, which scholars have struggled to locate.</p><p>Historians generally regard him as trustworthy and he cites his sources, though some claims rely on earlier writers, such as his identification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tel-dan">Laish</a>, Tel Dan, with Baniyas. His account preceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>'s descriptions of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> by a hundred years. It was translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and later most major European languages, and drew much attention from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> scholars in the 16th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Benjamin of Tudela” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Routes ran through the Bayuda Desert to Makuria, from Berber to the Red Sea ports of Badi, Suakin and Dahlak, and, <b>Benjamin of Tudela</b> wrote, west to Zuwila in Fezzan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Tudela’s Jewish community produced the twelfth-century traveller <b>Benjamin of Tudela</b>, whose account describes Jewish communities from Spain to Baghdad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in the Kingdom of Castile. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.07,-1.61&amp;year=1163" aria-label="Tudela on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Benjamin of Tudela"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4898 3975h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4898 3975h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.07,-1.61&amp;year=1163">What was here in 1163? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
